WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.870
Fiery feathers, venomous spit, and jaws that
can crush an SUV.

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:10.970
"Jurassic World Dominion" features some of
the biggest and baddest dinos of the franchise,

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.440
but only one can claim victory over the others…
and it's done it more than once.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.250
"Hold onto your butts."

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:22.119
Gallimimus is a long-necked egg-stealer whose
biggest claim to fame is probably being named

00:00:22.119 --> 00:00:24.817
by young Tim in the original "Jurassic Park."

00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:26.935
"Gala… uh…

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:29.375
Gallimimus!"

00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:33.630
It's a fine dinosaur, and its ostrich-like
appearance definitely sets it apart from its

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:38.020
similarly sized cousins, but unfortunately
that's about all that Gallimimus has going

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:39.020
for it.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.670
It isn't fearsome enough to get points in
the fright category, nor is it majestic enough

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.470
to rub shoulders with the sauropods, mostly
because those shoulders are way too high.

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:52.230
As the final nail in its coffin, Gallimimus
barely appears in "Jurassic World Dominion"

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.780
and provides little to the story or the thrills.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.810
Perhaps that's just karmic justice for all
the eggs they've stolen over the years.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:04.369
Our second-from-the-bottom spot is shared
by two different dinos alike in both size

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:09.240
and importance — the tiny Oviraptor and
equally small Moros intrepidus.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:14.009
Moros intrepidus' biggest moment in "Dominion"
is a single shot where it's shown nibbling

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:16.950
between the teeth of the massive Giganotosaurus.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:22.303
The little guy is cute, but he's used mostly
as a contrast to show how massive the Giganoto is.

00:01:22.303 --> 00:01:26.740
Oviraptor occupies about the same amount of
screen time, stealing an egg in the film's

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:32.235
non-theatrical prologue and making an even
less memorable cameo later on in a pit fight scene.

00:01:32.235 --> 00:01:37.390
Both Oviraptor and Moros intrepidus look cool,
but they don't stand up to the rest of the

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:42.230
dinos in the film, and they're too small to
make much of an impact, literally and figuratively.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:48.573
If both dinos got credits at the end of the
film, they'd probably come below craft services.

00:01:48.573 --> 00:01:51.850
On paper, Iguanodon is a pretty cool dinosaur.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.500
It's a beefy herbivore with a beak-like snout
that can move on either two legs or four.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.189
Unfortunately, "Jurassic World Dominion" doesn't
feature enough of the creature to earn it

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:01.570
a spot higher on this list.

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:06.259
The Iguanodon only appears in the film's non-theatrical
prologue which is set during the actual time

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.369
of the dinosaurs.

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:11.840
A group of them are shown grazing as two predators
approach, and they're never heard from again.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.261
If you're an Iguanodon fan who was disappointed
by its minimal presence in "Jurassic World

00:02:16.261 --> 00:02:17.480
Dominion," then you're in luck.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.440
The 2000 Disney animated feature "Dinosaur"
casts the dino in the starring role.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.760
Sure, the animation might be a little dated,
and the film didn't get rave reviews upon

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.750
its initial release, but a full theatrical
movie is more than most of the dinos here

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:33.282
have on their resumes.

00:02:33.282 --> 00:02:38.569
When you think of the most iconic "Jurassic
Park" dinosaurs, the chicken-sized Compsognathus

00:02:38.569 --> 00:02:42.670
may not be the first ones to come to mind,
but there's no denying that the little guys

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.810
belong on that short list as well.

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.390
From their horrific scene in "The Lost World:
Jurassic Park" to their numerous other appearances

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:53.700
throughout the franchise, Compsognathus is
a scary member of the Jurassic family.

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:57.550
They even get the better of John Hammond himself
in the original Michael Crichton novel after

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:02.520
Hammond breaks his ankle, attracting a large
pack of Compies that prey on the injured.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.360
Sadly, the Compies don't get to do much in
"Jurassic World Dominion," and their most

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.250
notable appearance is during a stray shot
in the underground Malta dino market.

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:14.660
Still, they earn a slightly higher spot in
our ranking for two reasons: the merits of

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:21.290
their legacy in the franchise and their unshakeable
status as the best tiny dino in the business.

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:25.700
A much smaller cousin of the Spinosaurus,
Baryonyx is a long-snouted dinosaur that can

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.980
grow to a pretty impressive size.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:31.590
The species makes an appearance in "Jurassic
World Dominion" fighting in the underground

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:35.779
dinosaur black market in Malta, where it's
seen battling in the pits against a young Allosaurus.

00:03:35.779 --> 00:03:40.590
While Baryonyx doesn't have much in the way
of looks or power to rank above more notable

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:44.959
predators, the fact that it can hold its own
in a one-on-one is definitely enough to bump

00:03:44.959 --> 00:03:46.730
it up at least a few spots.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:51.260
The Malta fighting pits are some of the most
brutal examples of human cruelty to dinosaurs

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.750
shown in "Jurassic World Dominion," and there
are a bunch.

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:59.230
That makes Baryonyx not only a cool dino in
its own right but also an underdog story that's

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.833
definitely worth rooting for.

00:04:00.833 --> 00:04:05.040
Maybe the one seen in Malta eventually makes
his way to the Dolomite sanctuary after the

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.328
dinos all escape.

00:04:06.328 --> 00:04:10.069
Flying dinosaurs are cool — they just are.

00:04:10.069 --> 00:04:13.650
They're basically the closest thing to dragons
Earth has ever seen.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:18.530
Sadly, while the Pteranodon is the most famous
example of a winged dinosaur, its status as

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:24.220
de facto king of the primordial skies is usurped
in "Jurassic World Dominion" by other cooler

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:25.319
flying dinosaurs.

00:04:25.319 --> 00:04:29.820
There are still a number of scenes that feature
the Pteranodon in all their glory, from their

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:34.060
bat-like wings to their long, skinny beaks
and pointy heads, but when compared to how

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:39.120
impressive the Pteranodons have been in previous
films, "Dominion" just falls a little short.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.340
A lesser known winged dinosaur — the Dimorphodon
— shares the Pteranodon's place on this

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:44.901
list for a couple reasons.

00:04:44.901 --> 00:04:49.880
While it is cool that the Dimorphodon's head
looks like a raptor or T-Rex, it doesn't get

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:53.580
to do nearly enough in the movie to justify
a higher position.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:58.320
Within the context of "Jurassic World: Dominion,"
both of these dinos serve about the same purpose

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:03.000
— flying around and looking cool without
contributing much to the story.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:09.211
Science shows that a spiky dinosaur is better
than a not-spiky dinosaur almost every time.

00:05:09.211 --> 00:05:15.470
Of those spiky reptiles, Stegosaurus and Ankylosaurus
are probably the two most famous.

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:19.780
Whether you prefer Stego's vertical spines
or the Ankylosaurus' spikes and club-like

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.120
tail, these are undeniably two of the most
iconic dinos known to man.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.828
It's fitting, then, that Ankylosaurus and
Stegosaurus share a spot on this list.

00:05:27.828 --> 00:05:32.289
If either species had a notable role in "Jurassic
World: Dominion," that would be enough to

00:05:32.289 --> 00:05:36.530
separate them, but they're both relegated
to the same fate — a handful of pretty vista

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.780
shots without any actual appearances in the
action.

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:44.039
For two less spectacular dinos, that would
be a cause for extinction, but because of

00:05:44.039 --> 00:05:48.701
how great both are in any context, they still
get to stay in the middle tier of our ranking.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:53.650
It also helps that both species feature in
such beautiful shots, which make their roles

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:57.620
memorable despite the lack of substantial
screen time.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.620
Lots of dinosaurs have intimidating appearances,
but only one of them has a name that means

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.520
"Demon of the River Styx."

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:10.240
Known for its bone-hard skull and its spikes,
Stygimoloch is certainly an easy dino to pick

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:11.100
out in a crowd.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:16.680
Even in the densely-packed Malta black market
full of illegally acquired species, Stygimoloch

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.060
sticks out like a sore thumb.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:22.660
While it doesn't get to do much in "Dominion,"
this dino does give an intimidating stare

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.840
toward all the humans who pass by its underground
pen.

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.690
We can only assume that it exacts some vengeance
during the mass dino prison break, using its

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:34.099
powerful skull to free its prehistoric comrades
from captivity.

00:06:34.099 --> 00:06:38.980
Stygimoloch gets some bonus points in "Jurassic
World: Dominion" for the hardships it endured,

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:43.669
as well as for simply being one of the coolest-looking
dinosaurs on the planet.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:46.021
Who doesn't love a sauropod?

00:06:46.021 --> 00:06:51.069
These long-necked, hump-backed, thick-legged
herbivores are some of the most iconic dinosaurs

00:06:51.069 --> 00:06:55.660
ever discovered, and they've always played
a major role in the "Jurassic" movies.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:00.370
Naturally majestic, they're often used to
cement the awe-inspiring nature of dinosaurs

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:04.860
in general, as in their famous debut in the
original "Jurassic Park."

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:07.765
"It's… it's a dinosaur."

00:07:07.765 --> 00:07:13.970
For number 17 on our dino ranking, we're lumping
two of the big leaf-eaters together: Apatosaurus

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:15.477
and Brachiosaurus.

00:07:15.477 --> 00:07:20.470
Apatosaurus might rank slightly higher here
since it gets a beautiful encounter with some

00:07:20.470 --> 00:07:24.310
workers at a lumber yard in "Jurassic World:
Dominion," but it doesn't do enough to separate

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.970
itself from the Brachio.

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:30.600
As the larger and more vertical of the two,
Brachiosaurus still cuts a more impressive

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.914
figure, even if it plays a slightly smaller
role this time around.

00:07:33.914 --> 00:07:39.805
Still, that early, snowy Apatosaurus scene
is undeniably impressive.

00:07:39.805 --> 00:07:45.600
At number 16 on our list, it's our big-boy
carnivore: Allosaurus.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.539
Smaller than a T-Rex but larger than a raptor,
the Allosaurus is most people's ideal of a

00:07:50.539 --> 00:07:55.484
prehistoric predator with big hind legs, small
arms, claws, jaws filled with pointy teeth,

00:07:55.484 --> 00:07:57.334
and a long, impressive tail.

00:07:57.334 --> 00:08:01.770
Allosaurus is also the first dinosaur on this
list to have a more prominent role in "Jurassic

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:02.598
World: Dominion."

00:08:02.598 --> 00:08:07.190
During the extended action sequence in Malta,
an Allosaurus and a Carnotaurus both escape

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:11.120
from an underground dino black market, leading
to a full-on brawl in the street.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:15.320
It's a cool moment that's enough on its own
to earn Allosaurus a ranking this high.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:20.060
Unfortunately, the Allosaurus is held back
from going any higher for one gigantic reason:

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:21.440
the Giganotosaurus.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.790
The two dinosaurs look remarkably similar,
with the Giganoto even being mistaken for

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:27.648
a large Allosaurus at one point.

00:08:27.648 --> 00:08:32.310
The comparison makes Allosaurus look a lot
less impressive and interesting, especially

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:36.219
given the huge role its doppelganger plays
later in the film.

00:08:36.219 --> 00:08:38.460
It's easy for a dinosaur to be terrifying.

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:42.830
From their massive, scaly bodies to their
razor-sharp teeth, instilling fear isn't something

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.733
that most species have to work at.

00:08:44.733 --> 00:08:49.470
Being cute, on the other hand, is a much more
daunting task for a dino.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:53.440
After all, how many carnivorous reptiles actually
make you want to snuggle?

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:57.209
There are two species worthy of that honor
in "Jurassic World Dominion."

00:08:57.209 --> 00:09:01.260
The first, Lystrosaurus, is seen in the Malta
black market.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:07.320
She's a cutie who basically looks like a squat
and scaly pig, with a little bit of a Triceratops-type

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:08.062
skull plate.

00:09:08.062 --> 00:09:12.299
She starts to snarl when all the action goes
down, but it only makes her more adorable.

00:09:12.299 --> 00:09:17.649
The other dinosaur tied for this spot, Microceratus,
is exactly what it sounds like — a small,

00:09:17.649 --> 00:09:20.200
triceratops-like creature with an adorable
face.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:25.209
While larger than the Lystrosaurus, Microceratus
also makes the audience squeal at its cuteness,

00:09:25.209 --> 00:09:28.852
offering a brief break from all the murderous
rampaging.

00:09:28.852 --> 00:09:33.229
"Yeah… that's nice… gotta go."

00:09:33.229 --> 00:09:38.279
No one has a longer head than Parasaurolophus,
which counts for something in itself.

00:09:38.279 --> 00:09:43.200
This chunky, largely peaceful dino has been
used as set dressing in past films, given

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.459
how majestic the species looks when moving
in a herd.

00:09:46.459 --> 00:09:50.430
That's still largely how the Parasaurolophus
is used in "Jurassic World Dominion," but

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:52.596
it gets a bit more time in the spotlight.

00:09:52.596 --> 00:09:56.899
Not only is it the dino in Owen's introductory
scene where he chases a pack of the creatures

00:09:56.899 --> 00:10:01.390
on horseback like a prehistoric cowboy — but
it also has a touching moment that's used

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:03.570
to help us sympathize with the dinosaurs.

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:07.950
After having a heck of a time wrestling one
of the herbivores to a standstill, Owen manages

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.420
to calm it with some sweet words and gentleness.

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:15.630
A final scene shows the Parasaurolophus running
alongside wild horses, helping us see them

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:18.000
as animals, not monsters.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.980
Who doesn't love a good sea monster?

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:25.340
After prominent turns in "Jurassic World"
and "Jurassic World: Fallen Kingdom," the

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:29.399
Mosasaurus takes a step back in importance
in "Dominion," but not enough to knock it

00:10:29.399 --> 00:10:30.829
too far down this list.

00:10:30.829 --> 00:10:36.190
There's simply nothing else like the Mosasaurus
— from its gaping maw and massive flippers

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:41.381
to the sheer terror it inflicts every time
it shows up on screen, it's a monster of a

00:10:41.381 --> 00:10:44.329
dino that's in 20,000 leagues of its own.

00:10:44.329 --> 00:10:48.500
Additionally, the few scenes that the Mosasaurus
does get in "Jurassic World Dominion" still

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:50.307
manage to take our breath away.

00:10:50.307 --> 00:10:54.680
It kicks the film into high gear at the very
start by attacking an innocent fishing trawler

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.081
in brutal fashion.

00:10:56.081 --> 00:11:01.210
In a nice contrast, it ends the film by swimming
peacefully by some ordinary whales.

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:05.820
The land-based dinosaurs may get more screen
time overall, but there's something unique

00:11:05.820 --> 00:11:10.329
about the ocean-based Mosasaurus that's still
unmatched.

00:11:10.329 --> 00:11:15.630
After menacing multiple dinosaurs in Disney's
"Dinosaur" and hunting human campers in the

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:20.279
animated series "Jurassic World: Camp Cretaceous",
Carnotaurus finally gets a chance to shine

00:11:20.279 --> 00:11:23.450
in live action when it appears in "Jurassic
World Dominion."

00:11:23.450 --> 00:11:28.029
A Carnotaurus escapes from the Malta black
market early in the film and quickly begins

00:11:28.029 --> 00:11:33.410
laying waste to the city, battling an Allosaurus
and devouring some innocent bystanders along

00:11:33.410 --> 00:11:34.009
the way.

00:11:34.009 --> 00:11:38.700
While it technically has the same basic role
as the Allosaurus, Carnotaurus gets a leg

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:39.928
up for two reasons.

00:11:39.928 --> 00:11:42.028
First, it just looks cooler.

00:11:42.028 --> 00:11:47.010
That blood-red color and the horns on its
head give the Carnotaurus a top-tier intimidation

00:11:47.010 --> 00:11:49.340
factor, even when compared to other carnivores.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:54.940
Second, it doesn't suffer from the Giganotosaurus
comparisons the same way that Allosaurus does.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:12:01.639
Fast, vicious, and just plain awesome, Carnotaurus
is a fearsome predator in its own right, and

00:12:01.639 --> 00:12:04.960
it deserves the number 12 spot on our list.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:10.320
On its best days, Triceratops easily gets
a top-five spot on a favorite dinosaur list.

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:13.677
It's got the look, the toughness, and the
celebrity status.

00:12:13.677 --> 00:12:18.000
Unfortunately for fans of the three-horned
species, Triceratops doesn't get much real

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.940
screen time in "Jurassic World Dominion."

00:12:19.940 --> 00:12:25.020
The good news is that some of the other, less
famous members of the ceratopsian family finally

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.290
get some well-deserved spotlight shone on
them in the film.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:32.720
Claire and her crew save a baby Nasutoceratops
— a species with only the two top horns

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:34.550
— from illegal dino breeders.

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:39.279
Later, a herd of adult Nasutoceratops return
the favor by taking down the pursuing dino

00:12:39.279 --> 00:12:41.870
breeders, allowing Claire and her team to
escape.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:47.429
A Sinoceratops — possessing just the faceplate
and rhino-like nose horn of the Triceratops

00:12:47.429 --> 00:12:51.910
— also shows up in the film, most prominently
in the closing montage that shows how different

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:55.100
dinosaurs are becoming part of Earth's modern
ecosystem.

00:12:55.100 --> 00:12:59.280
Some of the species are seen walking alongside
a herd of elephants across what appears to

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:03.730
be the African savanna, giving viewers a nice
little bit of majestic lumbering before the

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:05.120
credits roll.

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:10.410
The first dinosaur to make it into our top
10 is none other than Dreadnoughtus, a sauropod

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:13.029
of truly mammoth proportions.

00:13:13.029 --> 00:13:18.089
While it doesn't get as much individual attention
in "Jurassic World Dominion" as the Apatosaurus,

00:13:18.089 --> 00:13:21.139
it earns a higher spot because of its impressive
size.

00:13:21.139 --> 00:13:25.990
The fact that it's never appeared in all its
glory in the franchise before just makes its

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.190
scenes more significant.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:31.060
Simply put, the Dreadnoughtus is awe-inspiring.

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:35.519
It's one of the first dinosaurs that Dr. Alan
Grant and Dr. Ellie Sattler see during their

00:13:35.519 --> 00:13:41.460
flyover of Biosyn's Dolomite Mountains sanctuary,
and it's a perfect choice to drive home just

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:45.079
how big and impressive the whole operation
is.

00:13:45.079 --> 00:13:49.959
Introducing Dreadnoughtus feels like both
a nod and an attempt at one-upping the sauropod

00:13:49.959 --> 00:13:52.430
reveal in the first "Jurassic Park."

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.690
The effect is impressive — just not as magical
this time around.

00:13:56.690 --> 00:14:00.880
Typically, Pteranodons have been the masters
of the sky in the "Jurassic" franchise.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:05.250
In "Jurassic World Dominion," however, that
title is snatched and carried even higher

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:07.730
into the air by Quetzalcoatlus.

00:14:07.730 --> 00:14:13.380
Not only does the winged beast look positively
gnarly with its gargoyle-ish head and feathery,

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:17.750
clawed body, it also stars in one of the film's
most frightening moments.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:22.160
While flying over the Dolomite sanctuary,
Kayla, Owen, and Claire are thrown into a

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:27.820
terrifying airborne battle when a Quetzalcoatlus
chooses their airplane for an evening snack.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:32.870
The creature tears the vessel to pieces in
record time, forcing Claire to eject and Kayla

00:14:32.870 --> 00:14:35.769
and Owen to attempt a dangerous crash-landing.

00:14:35.769 --> 00:14:40.680
Everyone ends up surviving, but it's still
a heart-pounding ordeal that gives this flying

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:44.079
dinosaur a top spot on this list.

00:14:44.079 --> 00:14:49.050
Biosyn's Dolomite dinosaur sanctuary is home
to a number of more obscure species who've

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:51.709
never been seen before in the franchise.

00:14:51.709 --> 00:14:57.020
One of those is Dimetrodon, a creature that
precedes most proper dinosaurs, and who provides

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:01.220
a heavy scare to Alan Grant, Ellie Sattler,
and Maisie Lockwood in the film.

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:05.740
While escaping from Biosyn's facility, the
trio comes across a Dimetrodon in the tunnels,

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:09.800
and it very nearly brings their journey to
an abrupt and violent end.

00:15:09.800 --> 00:15:14.100
Ramsey and Ian Malcolm save them in the nick
of time, but it's still pretty close.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:18.810
Being the star of such a big action scene
is enough to net Dimetrodon a high spot on

00:15:18.810 --> 00:15:23.110
this list, but the species is boosted even
higher by its unique look.

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:28.079
Adequately described as a massive alligator
with the back fin of a Spinosaurus, it's an

00:15:28.079 --> 00:15:33.330
imposing creature that you definitely wouldn't
want to run into in a dark tunnel.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:37.490
The Dilophosaurus occupies a very unique space
in the world of "Jurassic Park."

00:15:37.490 --> 00:15:42.069
Despite its small size, it's one of the most
feared dinosaurs in the "Jurassic Park" franchise,

00:15:42.069 --> 00:15:45.910
largely because of the way it blinds Dennis
Nedry with its venom in the first film before

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:46.623
attacking him.

00:15:46.623 --> 00:15:51.959
Nedry is a pretty reprehensible villain, and
the desire to see him get what's coming to

00:15:51.959 --> 00:15:56.060
him is satisfied when he comes face to face
with a Dilophosaurus.

00:15:56.060 --> 00:16:01.459
From the Dilophosaurus' neck frills to the
terrifying goop it spews to its hoarse call,

00:16:01.459 --> 00:16:04.380
this species is extremely memorable.

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:08.590
All of those characteristics are present when
the creature returns in "Jurassic World Dominion"

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:11.700
to exact another batch of poetic justice.

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:16.220
When Lewis Dodgson — the Biosyn CEO who
originally commissioned Nedry to steal embryos

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:20.310
from the first Jurassic Park — tries to
escape his own fallen kingdom, he comes face

00:16:20.310 --> 00:16:22.770
to face with a group of dinos.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:28.570
And just like the man he once hired, Dodgson
meets his gruesome end at the claws of the

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:29.489
Dilophosaurus.

00:16:29.489 --> 00:16:34.709
It's a nice full-circle moment that earns
the dino a top spot on this list.

00:16:34.709 --> 00:16:39.850
After being the de facto dinosaur protagonist
of the prior two films, Blue the Velociraptor

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:42.810
takes a surprising back seat in "Jurassic
World Dominion."

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.970
She has a decent amount of screen time at
the beginning of the film, but she doesn't

00:16:45.970 --> 00:16:47.830
join in any of the action later.

00:16:47.830 --> 00:16:51.990
Perhaps the filmmakers figured that people
were sick of Velociraptors always being the

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:58.419
smartest and most dangerous dinosaurs in the
room, making Blue decide to take a long-overdue vacation.

00:16:58.419 --> 00:17:00.336
"Clever girl."

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:05.190
Regardless, the Velociraptor as a species
still earns the number seven spot on this

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:07.600
list because of Blue's child, Beta.

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:12.240
While undeniably still a threat, Beta is more
cute than scary, and the little dino has plenty

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:13.870
of great moments throughout the film.

00:17:13.870 --> 00:17:18.250
From learning how to hunt from its momma to
escaping from Biosyn's laboratories with Maisie,

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:21.761
Beta shows us a different side of the Velociraptors
that feels fresh and entertaining.

00:17:21.761 --> 00:17:25.870
And did we mention how cute Beta is?

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:30.880
Despite being the big bad of "Jurassic World:
Dominion," the Giganotosaurus barely squeezes

00:17:30.880 --> 00:17:32.500
its way into our top five.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:37.490
Before we get to what's holding it back, let's
focus on what's great about this dinosaur.

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:42.640
A larger predator than even the Tyrannosaurus
rex, Giganotosaurus definitely has some wow

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:45.490
factor due to its sheer size.

00:17:45.490 --> 00:17:49.500
It also has a number of exciting moments,
from its showdown with the humans, where Ian

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:54.473
Malcolm throws a flaming spear in its mouth,
to its duel with the T-Rex in the movie's climax.

00:17:54.473 --> 00:18:00.310
It absolutely accomplishes what a "Jurassic"
villain dino needs to do — look big and

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:01.610
scary and trash everything.

00:18:01.610 --> 00:18:02.610
"Bigger.

00:18:02.725 --> 00:18:06.475
Why do they always have to go bigger?"

00:18:06.475 --> 00:18:10.980
Unfortunately, that's also the problem with
the Giganotosaurus.

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:15.450
For all of the buildup that it receives, it
doesn't really do anything that we haven't

00:18:15.450 --> 00:18:16.730
already seen in the franchise.

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:21.350
It's as if the writers took some of the T-Rex's
best scenes from all of the past films and

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:24.960
just swapped in a slightly larger, less famous
dinosaur.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:29.580
As a result, even Giganoto's most exciting
moments are hindered by a feeling of "been

00:18:29.580 --> 00:18:30.580
there, done that."

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:34.820
By the time the T-Rex shows back up to take
it on, you'll probably catch yourself cheering

00:18:34.820 --> 00:18:38.570
— not because you're so excited about the
coming battle, but because the movie finally

00:18:38.570 --> 00:18:41.880
gave you the carnivores you actually wanted
to see.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:46.710
Clocking in at number four is the dinosaur
that largely steals the Velociraptor's thunder,

00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:48.321
its cousin, the Atrociraptor.

00:18:48.321 --> 00:18:53.870
Seen solely in the Malta sequence, Biosyn's
purebred Atrociraptors are some of the fastest

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:57.780
and most lethal dinosaurs ever seen in a "Jurassic"
movie.

00:18:57.780 --> 00:19:02.850
The recipients of intensive training, they
go after Owen and Claire with lethal intensity,

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:08.669
leading to a tense chase scene where the dinos
reveal they can run just as fast as motorcycles and trucks.

00:19:08.669 --> 00:19:10.829
"Must go faster."

00:19:11.890 --> 00:19:16.620
The Malta pursuit is definitely one of the
standout moments in "Jurassic World Dominion,"

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:19.240
and that's mostly because of the Atrociraptors.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:22.950
It's also nice to see the franchise find a
way to put a new twist on the timeless appeal

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:26.450
of the Velociraptors, even if it only lasts
for a few minutes.

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:30.190
That being said, the Atrociraptor isn't the
best "Dominion" dino to bear the raptor family

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:32.020
name on this list, but more on that later..

00:19:32.020 --> 00:19:38.210
Red, feathered, and wielding some truly impressive
claws, Therizinosaurus is one of the most

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:42.090
unique and interesting dinosaurs in "Jurassic
World: Dominion."

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:47.789
From its quiet stalking of Claire in the Dolomites
to its accidental victory over the Giganotosaurus,

00:19:47.789 --> 00:19:53.090
Therizinosaurus leaves a lasting impression,
which is probably why it was featured so heavily

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:54.374
in the film's trailers.

00:19:54.374 --> 00:20:00.000
There's simply never been a dinosaur like
the Therizinosaurus in a "Jurassic" movie before.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.900
It looks like what Freddy Krueger would become
if he were a prehistoric bird, which is enough

00:20:04.900 --> 00:20:07.350
to make it the stuff of nightmares.

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:12.000
Then there's the fact that it's featured in
such a prominent role during the film's final act.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.730
As fun as it is to see classic dinos like
the T-rex and the Triceratops, it's arguably

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:21.549
even more fun to see a lesser-known species
used in a new and creative way.

00:20:21.549 --> 00:20:27.200
That's exactly what the Therizinosaurus delivers
in "Dominion," earning it the bronze medal.

00:20:27.200 --> 00:20:31.789
There's so much to love about the Pyroraptor
that it's honestly hard to know where to start.

00:20:31.789 --> 00:20:36.320
For starters, it has a rad name that's perfectly
suited to its fiery feathers.

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:41.289
Second, it stars in arguably the most exciting
dino encounter in "Jurassic World: Dominion."

00:20:41.289 --> 00:20:46.170
Facing off against a raptor of any sort with
nothing more than knives and a taser to defend

00:20:46.170 --> 00:20:50.611
yourself is already a tough task, but doing
it on a sheet of thin ice?

00:20:50.611 --> 00:20:52.380
That's just ridiculous.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:57.510
The Pyroraptor makes a big impression with
its sharp cries and incredible speed, but

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:00.604
the fact that it can swim makes it even cooler.

00:21:00.604 --> 00:21:03.268
"What an a—hole"

00:21:03.268 --> 00:21:06.539
Sadly, the Pyroraptor is a one-and-done dino.

00:21:06.539 --> 00:21:10.850
It would have been cool to see him show up
later like the Therizinosaurus, but maybe

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:14.340
that wouldn't have fit into an already packed
third act.

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:18.370
Nevertheless, the Pyroraptor easily takes
home the silver medal, as well as the title

00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:22.720
of best raptor in the entirety of "Jurassic
World Dominion."

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:24.610
Did you expect something different?

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:30.825
"Jurassic Park" has tried many times to replace
the T-Rex as the biggest and baddest dinosaur around.

00:21:30.825 --> 00:21:33.640
Suffice it to say, that's never worked out.

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:39.240
From its hulking skull to its tiny arms and
iconic roar, there's just too much to love

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:42.110
about the Tyrannosaurus for it to ever be
usurped.

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:47.169
Rexy doesn't even have that much screen time
in "Jurassic World Dominion," yet she dominates

00:21:47.169 --> 00:21:48.960
every second of it.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:54.640
Her crowning moment is her climactic duel
with the Giganotosaurus — an absolute slugfest

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:58.190
of mammoth proportions that decimates the
surrounding area.

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:02.240
And while the T-Rex does seem to go down for
the count at one point, she gets by with a

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:03.670
little help from her friends.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:08.610
A tiny distraction is all she needs to get
back on her feet and deliver a brutal body

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:12.990
blow to her opponent, knocking the Giganoto
directly into the talons of the Therizinosaurus

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:14.218
and impaling it.

00:22:14.218 --> 00:22:20.648
Rexy roars her trademark roar as the world
burns around her, declaring she's still, unequivocally,

00:22:20.648 --> 00:22:22.225
the queen.

